<section class="block">
<h2>Who this is for</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Founders and scale-ups</strong> in London and across the UK who need senior technical leadership for architecture, hiring and fundraising.</li>
<li><strong>Enterprises and public-sector suppliers</strong> taking AI from pilot to production under close scrutiny.</li>
<li><strong>Health and life sciences teams</strong> working with clinical and research data.</li>
<li><strong>Investors</strong> who need technical due diligence they can rely on.</li>
</ul>
</section>
<section class="block">
<h2>What shapes technology decisions in the UK</h2>
<p>UK GDPR remains the foundation, now amended by the Data (Use and Access) Act 2025, which received Royal Assent in June 2025 and is being brought into force in stages<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>. The ICO's guidance on AI and data protection sets out how it interprets the law for AI systems, including fairness, transparency, data minimisation and individual rights<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>. For architecture, that means knowing exactly what personal data each model sees, why, and how a decision can be explained or contested.</p>
<table class="tbl"><thead><tr><th>Rule or standard</th><th>Who it touches</th><th>What it means for architecture</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>UK GDPR and DUAA 2025</td><td>Anyone processing personal data in the UK</td><td>Data mapping, lawful basis per purpose, and safeguards for automated decisions designed into workflows</td></tr><tr><td>ICO guidance on AI</td><td>Organisations training or deploying AI on personal data</td><td>DPIAs, data minimisation, security testing and explainability evidence</td></tr><tr><td>Security expectations</td><td>Suppliers to enterprise and public sector</td><td>Baseline controls, logging and incident response that buyers' assessments expect</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>Related reading: <a href="/insights/prompt-injection-defence-for-agents/">prompt injection defence</a>, <a href="/insights/llm-evaluation-sets-that-predict-production/">evaluation sets that predict production</a> and <a href="/insights/iso-42001-vs-nist-ai-rmf/">ISO 42001 vs NIST AI RMF</a>.</p>
</section>
<section class="block">
<h2>Typical first projects</h2>
<ol>
<li>An AI readiness review that maps data, risks and the evidence a DPIA needs.</li>
<li>Moving a RAG or agent pilot into production with evaluations and guardrails.</li>
<li>Platform and cloud cost reset: Terraform, Kubernetes and FinOps.</li>
<li>Fractional CTO cover through a funding round or leadership gap.</li>
</ol>
</section>
<section class="block">
<h2>Time zones</h2>
<p>The UK working day overlaps comfortably with Gulf hours and the US East Coast morning, so UK teams get same-day answers and joint sessions with US or Middle East colleagues are easy to schedule.</p>
</section>
<section class="block">
<h2>How engagements work</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Architecture and AI readiness review</strong> (2 to 4 weeks): a clear picture of the current platform, risks and a prioritised roadmap.</li>
<li><strong>Fractional CTO</strong> (ongoing, part-time): technical strategy, architecture governance, hiring and board-level communication.</li>
<li><strong>Build and hand-off</strong>: hands-on delivery of a platform or AI capability, documented so your team can run it.</li>
<li><strong>Technical due diligence</strong>: an independent view of a target's technology for investors and acquirers.</li>
</ul>
<p>Every engagement starts with a free 30-minute call. See <a href="/how-i-work/">how I work</a> for the full process.</p>
</section>
